I'm in the process of recording my breastfeeding story to share with my wonderful doula pals. Each time I talk about feeding Margot, I get stuck on a certain point. Absorbed in remembering a different stage. 
 
This time, I have become fixated on the idea of this lost new mother, scared and confused and lacking any sort of professional help, attempting to breastfeed her tongue-tied baby, whilst expressing for a top-up feed and giving this bottle of expressed milk knowing that babies can develop a preference for the bottle, but that not pumping enough could impact on a fragile supply and leave the baby hungry. What I would do to go back, scoop her up and tell her 'I see how hard this is, I see your fear and I feel your unfounded sense of failure. I see you'.
 
It wasn’t sustainable. I saw no end point. I relished the power I felt in making the decision to pump exclusively. I told myself I would do it for a year. I researched obsessively. Pumping schedules. When I could drop the second night time pump. How to build a stash. Enough in the fridge to not panic about the next few feeds. How to maximise the effectiveness of each pumping session. Flange size. Power pumping to mimic cluster feeding. It goes on and on.
 
But my god it was TOUGH. Pumping whilst looking after Margot alone. Pumping whilst out. Explaining myself. Setting alarms. Endless alarms. Pumping in the night. Bottle feeding responsively. Paced bottle feeding. Safe storage and preparation of milk. Sterilization of bottles. Silently dealing with my grief of not feeding at my breast.
 
So if that's you now, I see you. If that was you, I see you. If you managed for a week, I see you. If you continued for months and years, I see you. It's so fucking tough. Please, reach out if you need and let someone hold you emotionally in the way I needed
 
